TEMPORARY diagnostic patch, RESTORE_CORRUPTION_PLAN3.md Phase A
("the scribble test" for the uninitialized-memory / stale-block
hypothesis). Not meant to ship -- delete once the restore-corruption
investigation (docs/kv-offload-cache-identity-corruption.md) closes.

Plan 2 established: a corrupted restore's checkpoint bytes are byte-
faithful all the way from store through load to GPU-read (no data-
movement bug), yet the exact same conversation's checkpoint restores
*clean* when it was originally primed with the GPU KV cache pool
otherwise empty, and *corrupts* when other conversations had previously
occupied the pool -- and the live (non-restored) reply is always correct
regardless. Plan 3's hypothesis: the stored checkpoint page contains a
byte range some write path never touches, and the live compute path never
reads that range either (so its correctness is unaffected) -- but the
restore path (which loads and consumes the *entire* stored page) does
read it. On a virgin GPU allocation that unread range is zero (CUDA
allocates already-zeroed pages); on a block recycled from a finished
conversation, it is that conversation's leftover bytes.

This patch adds `VLLM_DEBUG_SCRIBBLE_KV`: when set, every KV-cache tensor
is filled with a deliberately nonzero byte pattern (0x3C, or 0xFF via
`VLLM_DEBUG_SCRIBBLE_KV=ff`) immediately after allocation, in
`GPUModelRunner.initialize_kv_cache()` -- right after
`initialize_kv_cache_tensors()` returns and before the KV-transfer group
(offload connector) ever registers or touches them, so every byte in
every KV-cache tensor is guaranteed nonzero before any request runs. This
turns "reads a never-written byte" from "silently reads zero" (which a
virgin CUDA allocation supplies "for free", making a real bug and a
non-bug indistinguishable) into "detectably reads garbage" -- if the
hypothesis is right, priming a single, otherwise-uncontended conversation
against a scribbled pool should now corrupt on restore too, even though
plan 2's identical recipe against an *unscribbled* pool restored clean
3/3.

`VLLM_DEBUG_SCRIBBLE_KV_ONLY` (values: "attn", "mamba"; unset = all
groups) restricts scribbling to one side of the attention/Mamba split,
for Phase B's field bisection -- classification is by
`kv_cache_config.kv_cache_groups[i].kv_cache_spec` (FullAttentionSpec vs.
everything else, i.e. Mamba/GDN's MambaSpec in this deployment).

Verification: applies cleanly (bash verify.sh --no-server); with
VLLM_DEBUG_SCRIBBLE_KV unset, behavior is unchanged (the hook is a no-op
gated by `if not _SCRIBBLE_KV: return`). With it set, `grep SCRIBBLE_KV
qwen.log` shows one line per KV-cache layer plus a summary line at
startup, before any request is served.

